2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] Found it. The `-50` (`paramErr`) is coming from the `AudioStreamBasicDescription` endianness bug in
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] `macosaudio.ts`.
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] In `createAudioStreamDescription()` you have:
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] view.setUint32(8, 0x6c70636d, false);
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] `mFormatID` is a numeric FourCC. On Apple Silicon / x86_64 macOS, the field must be written in
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] native/little-endian order, otherwise CoreAudio reads it as `0x6d63706c` instead of
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] `'lpcm'` and rejects the format.
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] Change it to:
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] view.setUint32(8, 0x6c70636d, true);
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] Or equivalently:
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] const kAudioFormatLinearPCM = 0x6c70636d; // 'lpcm'
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] view.setUint32(8, kAudioFormatLinearPCM, true);
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] The rest of your ASBD fields are already little-endian. That should get past `AudioQueueNewOutput`.
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] If it still returns `-50` after that, the next suspect is passing `null` for the output callback.
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] Some queue configurations require a non-null `AudioQueueOutputCallback`. You can supply a no-op
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] callback with `Deno.UnsafeCallback` and keep it alive for the lifetime of the queue.
2026-08-09 15:51:42 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:35 [simon@midnightblue.local] a dummy callback???????
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 35.48KB 13.80s
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] You're right to be surprised — but no, I meant a real no-op function, not `null`. Apple's
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] `AudioQueueNewOutput` requires a callback pointer:
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] OSStatus AudioQueueNewOutput(
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     const AudioStreamBasicDescription *inFormat,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     AudioQueueOutputCallback inCallbackProc,   // required, cannot be NULL
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     void *inUserData,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     CFRunLoopRef inCallbackRunLoop,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     CFStringRef inCallbackRunLoopMode,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     UInt32 inFlags,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash]     AudioQueueRef *outAQ
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] );
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] Passing `NULL` for `inCallbackProc` is one cause of `-50` (`paramErr`). Even if you never need the
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] callback, you still have to hand the API a valid function pointer. In Deno that means creating a
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] no-op `UnsafeCallback` and keeping it alive.
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] But the first bug is the endianness on `mFormatID`. You wrote `view.setUint32(8, 0x6c70636d,
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] false)` — that's big-endian. All other fields are little-endian, and CoreAudio on macOS expects
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] `mFormatID` in native/little-endian order. So `'lpcm'` is being read as garbage.
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] Fix both:
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] // near the top of macosaudio.ts
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] let noOpCallback: Deno.UnsafeCallback | null = null;
2026-08-09 15:56:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
