2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.005 654B 5.92s
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Commodore PET keyboard operates on the same fundamental matrix scanning principle as the VIC-20
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] and C64, but there are a few key differences in the hardware implementation and matrix dimensions:
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Matrix Dimensions (10 × 8 vs. 8 × 8)
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] • VIC-20 & C64: Both use an 8-row by 8-column matrix (64 distinct switch points).
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] • PET: Uses a 10-row by 8-column matrix (80 possible switch points).
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Hardware Interface
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] • VIC-20 / C64: Directly drive 8 output lines and read 8 input lines using two 8-bit
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] parallel ports (via the 6522 VIAs on VIC-20 or 6526 CIAs on C64).
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] • PET: Uses a 74145 BCD-to-Decimal Decoder connected to PIA 1 (6520). The CPU
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] outputs a 4-bit binary value (0–9) to select one of the 10 rows (active low), and reads the 8
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] return lines via the other PIA port.
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Multiple Keyboard Variants
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] Unlike the relatively uniform physical layouts of the VIC-20 and C64, the PET family has two
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] distinct layouts with completely different matrix mappings:
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Graphics Keyboard (Chiclet & Full-Travel):
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Found on the original PET 2001 and educational models (e.g., 4016/4032).
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Features dedicated numeric/cursor pads and graphic symbols printed directly on keys.
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Business Keyboard:
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Introduced with the PET 2001-N / 3000 / 8000 series.
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Resembles a standard typewriter layout with punctuation shifted, dedicated lowercase support,
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] and lacks the PETSCII graphics on keytops.
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Requires a different matrix table and corresponding ROM (Editor ROM).
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] Summary for Simulator Implementation
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] If you extend your current matrix implementation to accommodate a 10×8 grid and route the row
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] selection through a 4-bit input rather than a direct 8-bit bitmask, you will be able to reuse most
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] of your existing matrix polling logic. You will also want to support switching keymaps depending on
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] whether the system is configured as a Graphics or Business PET.
2026-08-18 19:06:56 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:16:15 [simon@midnightblue.local] which bits of e810 or e812 select the matrix row to scan?
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.004 2.844KB 4.30s
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] The keyboard row is selected using Bits 0–3 of `$E812` (PIA 1, Port B).
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] How the Ports Are Assigned:
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$E812` (PIA 1 Port B): Matrix Row Output
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Bits 0–3 (`PB0`–`PB3`): Provide the 4-bit binary/BCD value (0 to 9) fed into the 74145
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] decoder to pull down one of the 10 matrix rows.
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Bits 4–7: Reserved for other hardware functions (e.g., cassette motor controls and
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] screen blanking/retrace signals, depending on the board revision).
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$E810` (PIA 1 Port A): Matrix Column Input
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Bits 0–7 (`PA0`–`PA7`): Read the 8 column lines returning from the keyboard matrix. A
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] bit reads as `0` (low) when the key at the intersection of the active row and that column is
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] pressed.
2026-08-18 19:16:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
