2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 24.33KB 2.18s
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, opcode `$5E` is also a valid, official NMOS 6502 opcode.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] Opcode `$5E` (decimal 94)
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Instruction: `LSR` (Logical Shift Right)
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Addressing Mode: Absolute, X-indexed (`$aaaa,X`)
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Byte Length: 3 bytes (`5E LL HH`)
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Cycles: 7 cycles (fixed cycle count because it is a read-modify-write instruction)
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Operation: Shifts the memory byte at `(HHLL + X)` right by 1 bit.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Bit 0 shifts into the Carry flag (C).
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Bit 7 is cleared to `0`.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Flags affected:
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]     * N (Negative): Always set to `0`.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]     * Z (Zero): Set if result is `0`, cleared otherwise.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash]     * C (Carry): Holds the old bit 0.
2026-08-18 21:08:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:41 [simon@midnightblue.local] does pet key repeat depend on hw timer?
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.011 25.23KB 7.16s
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] • No, key repeat does not use a dedicated hardware timer. It is handled entirely in
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] software* via countdown counters inside the standard 60 Hz system interrupt handler
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] (`IRQ`).
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] How Key Repeat Works in PET Kernal / Hardware
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. The 60 Hz IRQ Tick:
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * An interrupt is triggered 60 times per second (50 Hz on PAL models), generated by the CRT
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] vertical blank signal wired to the 6520 PIA 1 (`CB1`/`CA1` line) or 6522 VIA.
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * On each interrupt tick, the Kernal calls the keyboard scan routine (`SCNKEY`).
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Software Repeat Counters:
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * When a key is held down across multiple IRQ ticks, the Kernal decrements software delay
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] countdown bytes in low RAM:
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]      * Initial Delay Counter: Waits for a hold delay (typically ~16–30 ticks / ~0.5
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] seconds).
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]      * Repeat Speed Counter: Once active, pushes the key into the keyboard buffer every 4–6
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] ticks.
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * If `IRQ` ticks stop occurring, key repeats and keyboard buffering stop functioning.
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. PET Graphics vs. Business Models:
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Graphics Keyboard (PET 2001 / 3000): Only certain keys (or all keys depending on
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] Kernal version and the repeat flag at memory location `$028A` / `650`) repeat automatically in
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] software.
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Business Keyboard (4000 / 8000 series): Has a dedicated physical `RPT` key
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] wired directly into the keyboard matrix. Holding a key down together with `RPT` tells the software
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] scan routine to bypass the initial delay and repeat immediately.
2026-08-18 21:10:49 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
