2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 503B 4.27s
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] The short answer is: not natively on the earliest models, but yes on later revisions and via
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] simple hardware additions.
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] Here is how audio works across the Commodore PET line:
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Original PET 2001 (1977):
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash]   These models did not include an internal speaker or dedicated sound chip. However, the system's
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] MOS 6522 VIA (Versatile Interface Adapter) chip could generate tones using its shift
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] register output on pin CB2. Users commonly connected an external piezo buzzer or amplifier to the
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] User Port (pins CB2 and Ground) to get 1-channel sound in games and utilities.
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Later PET / CBM Models (2001-N, 4000, 8000 series):
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Later revisions included a built-in piezo beeper connected internally to the 6522 VIA. While it
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] was primarily intended for system beeps and alerts, software could control the frequency and output
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] simple one-voice melodies or basic sound effects.
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Sound Capabilities:
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash]   The audio is limited to simple square-wave tones (or rudimentary sample playback through fast
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] software bit-banging), as the PET does not have a dedicated synthesizer chip like the SID chip
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] found in the later Commodore 64.
2026-08-18 21:44:11 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
